<script>
  import CauseTable from '#lib/kb/CauseTable.svelte';
  import Callout from '#lib/kb/Callout.svelte';
  import RenderStamp from '#lib/kb/RenderStamp.svelte';
  import reports from '#lib/data/reports-4471.json';
  import notes from '#lib/data/desk-notes.json';
  import { groupByCause, readAs, uniqueById } from '#lib/kb/causes';
  import { tenantOf } from '#lib/kb/locales';

  // The desk's own view, as exported: one id, one report; cause by tenant and channel.
  const merged = uniqueById(reports).filter((r) => r.mergedInto === 4471);
  const groups = groupByCause(merged, tenantOf);
  const inGroup = (cause) => groups.find((g) => g.cause === cause);
  // The desk-noted one-day reports are footnoted under their cause; here they get their own row.
  const moved = (cause) => readAs(inGroup(cause), notes.oneDayMove);
  const parser = inGroup('parser').reports.length - moved('parser');
  const hint = inGroup('hint').reports.length - moved('hint');
  const oneDay = groups.reduce((n, g) => n + readAs(g, notes.oneDayMove), 0);
</script>

The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says the reports are one issue with one fix queued. Its own
table shows more than one cause. Read report by report, #4471 holds three problems. Each one now
has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes the first problem only. The other two keep coming after it ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<p><strong>{merged.length}</strong> reports merged into #4471, one ticket each:</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>problem</th><th>reports</th><th>ticket</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>parser</code> — day and month swapped</td><td>{parser}</td><td><a href="https://github.com/Yogelo/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/1">#1</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>hint</code> — booking-v3 form asks month first</td><td>{hint}</td><td><a href="https://github.com/Yogelo/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/2">#2</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>one-day move</code> — date lands one day off</td><td>{oneDay}</td><td><a href="https://github.com/Yogelo/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/3">#3</a></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

## What #4471 holds, as the desk files it

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The desk files the one-day reports under `parser` by tenant and footnotes them. In the split above
they leave the `parser` row and get their own ticket, so each report sits in one ticket.

## The three problems

**`parser` — day and month swapped.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every
tenant on file writes the day first, so 3 May becomes 5 March. This is the fix that is queued.
Ticket [#1](https://github.com/Yogelo/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/1).

**`hint` — the booking-v3 form asks for the wrong order.** Under its date field, booking-v3
prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. People type month first, as the hint tells them, and the form reads the
tenant's way, day first. Only form reports from tenants on booking-v3. The parser fix does not
touch it: the form needs a day-first hint. The table marks it "no ticket of its own"; it has one
now: [#2](https://github.com/Yogelo/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/2).

**`one-day move` — the date lands one day off.** These reports describe a date one day early,
an invite sent before the booking, and two clients in Lisbon and Toronto who got different days
for one meeting. That is a time zone problem, not a swap. The desk noted them
(`desk-notes.json`). Ticket [#3](https://github.com/Yogelo/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/3).

## How the count was made

- The export repeats some rows. One id, one report (`uniqueById`).
- Cause by tenant and channel, as in `causes.ts`. `intakeTag` is not the cause: intake tags
  `hint` whenever the reporter's browser is `en-US`.
- The desk-noted one-day reports (`readAs`) are taken out of their cause and counted once, in #3.
- Every number on this page is computed from the export at render time.

## What changes on the desk

- Merging on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put all three
  problems in one ticket, because their subjects match. Check the cause before you merge.
- Close #4471 against the parser fix only. Keep #2 and #3 open.

